WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.360
&gt;&gt; Welcome, everyone,
and good morning.

00:00:07.361 --> 00:00:10.600
So, it's my pleasure
to welcome back

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:15.130
Frederik Kjolstad back to MSR.

00:00:15.131 --> 00:00:18.020
He is a grad student in MIT.

00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:20.770
He's working with
Saman Amarasinghe,

00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:23.380
and he is in
the field of Compiler

00:00:23.381 --> 00:00:27.250
and Compiler Optimizations and
High-Performance Computing.

00:00:27.251 --> 00:00:30.235
And he has been working on

00:00:30.236 --> 00:00:35.455
linear algebra for
dense and sparse Inputs.

00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:38.380
And he's going to talk
about his recent compiler,

00:00:38.381 --> 00:00:43.950
which is about
Tensor Optimization.

00:00:43.951 --> 00:00:44.520
&gt;&gt;Thanks Seth.

00:00:44.521 --> 00:00:46.230
So last, year about a year ago,

00:00:46.231 --> 00:00:47.690
I tend to talk
about a programming

00:00:47.691 --> 00:00:49.355
language we have called SEMAT,

00:00:49.356 --> 00:00:51.860
which is for
computing on graphs or

00:00:51.861 --> 00:00:54.880
sparse systems using
linear algebra.

00:00:54.881 --> 00:00:56.345
As part of the future work,

00:00:56.346 --> 00:00:57.920
I talked about the need for

00:00:57.921 --> 00:01:00.800
a strong compiler for compiling
sparse linear algebra,

00:01:00.801 --> 00:01:02.885
intenser algebra especially when

00:01:02.886 --> 00:01:07.035
the matrices and
vectors are blocked.

00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:09.570
I'm back today to
talk about that work.

00:01:09.571 --> 00:01:11.270
So, we have done it
in the last year,

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:13.692
and we also made it
work for tensor algebra,

00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:15.484
generalized tensor
algebra as well,

00:01:15.485 --> 00:01:17.165
which is really neat.

00:01:17.166 --> 00:01:20.150
And it's dense and
sparse tensor algebra.

00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:23.875
So, we implemented
this theory in our compiler.

00:01:23.876 --> 00:01:25.405
So, I'm going to
present the theory.

00:01:25.406 --> 00:01:26.750
I'm implementing
a compiler called

00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:30.390
the Tensor Algebra Compiler
which we abbreviate to TACO.

00:01:30.391 --> 00:01:32.295
And this is joint work with

00:01:32.296 --> 00:01:34.400
Stephen Chou and
Saman Amarasinghe

00:01:34.401 --> 00:01:34.570
who both of them are at MIT.

00:01:34.571 --> 00:01:37.615
And Shoaib Kamil who at Adobe,

00:01:37.616 --> 00:01:39.208
and then David Lugato,

00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:42.030
who is at the French
Alternative Energies

00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:44.630
and Atomic Energy Commission.

00:01:44.631 --> 00:01:48.380
So, there is a lot

00:01:48.381 --> 00:01:50.885
of tensors around
everywhere these days.

00:01:50.886 --> 00:01:52.640
You will find them
in data analytics

00:01:52.641 --> 00:01:53.785
where you have things like;

00:01:53.786 --> 00:01:55.760
the Netflix movie ratings,

00:01:55.761 --> 00:01:57.965
or the Amazon product reviews,

00:01:57.966 --> 00:02:00.283
or the social
interactions of Facebook.

00:02:00.284 --> 00:02:02.090
And a tensor is

00:02:02.091 --> 00:02:05.545
a generalization of a matrix
to any number of dimensions.

00:02:05.546 --> 00:02:07.545
So, a vector would
be a zero tensor,

00:02:07.546 --> 00:02:09.245
a matrix would be
a three tensor,

00:02:09.246 --> 00:02:11.400
and then you could have
three tensors like this,

00:02:11.401 --> 00:02:13.400
or four tensors and so on.

00:02:13.401 --> 00:02:15.600
You also find them in
machine learning these days,

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.330
where both images and

00:02:17.331 --> 00:02:20.994
these different inputs
can be sort of a tensor.

00:02:20.995 --> 00:02:23.960
Or that operations
can also be a tensor.

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:25.215
So a convolutional layer,

00:02:25.216 --> 00:02:26.800
applying that
convolutional layers can

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:28.184
be a tensor operation.

00:02:28.185 --> 00:02:30.350
Or applying a fully
connected layer

00:02:30.351 --> 00:02:32.585
can be of dense
tensor operation.

00:02:32.586 --> 00:02:34.670
Or, it can be a sparse
tensor operation if

00:02:34.671 --> 00:02:36.725
you are talking about
sparse neural networks.

00:02:36.726 --> 00:02:38.210
But you also find a lot of them

00:02:38.211 --> 00:02:40.260
in science and engineering.

00:02:40.261 --> 00:02:41.990
Tensors made it possible for

00:02:41.991 --> 00:02:44.120
Einstein to do
his general relativity.

00:02:44.121 --> 00:02:45.540
And they used all over the place

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:47.755
in quantum chromodynamics,

00:02:47.756 --> 00:02:50.365
quantum mechanics in general,

00:02:50.366 --> 00:02:52.595
and they also used
in other places.

00:02:52.596 --> 00:02:54.655
Here, I have the
finite element method.

00:02:54.656 --> 00:02:55.925
And the key there is that,

00:02:55.926 --> 00:02:57.275
a lot of that is matrices,

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:58.580
sometimes you use tensors and

00:02:58.581 --> 00:03:01.075
these kind of
structural mechanics.

00:03:01.076 --> 00:03:02.990
But since we are talking

00:03:02.991 --> 00:03:05.045
about the generalizations
of matrices,

00:03:05.046 --> 00:03:06.355
we also include linear algebra.

00:03:06.356 --> 00:03:07.400
So, all I've been
talking about the

00:03:07.401 --> 00:03:08.940
Parser linear algebra as well.

00:03:08.941 --> 00:03:11.260
So we support that too.

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:14.925
So, let's look at this
Amazon product review tensor.

00:03:14.926 --> 00:03:16.770
So, in the first dimension,

00:03:16.771 --> 00:03:18.420
this tensor has users.

00:03:18.421 --> 00:03:21.200
So different names of users
and there's many users.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:22.220
It is a huge tensor.

00:03:22.221 --> 00:03:24.860
Then it's a three tensor,

00:03:24.861 --> 00:03:27.195
it has a second dimension
and that would be products.

00:03:27.196 --> 00:03:29.985
So here is a different products
that users bought.

00:03:29.986 --> 00:03:31.340
In the third dimension, you have

00:03:31.341 --> 00:03:34.200
the words in
the product reviews.

00:03:34.201 --> 00:03:36.030
Prior work has shown that

00:03:36.031 --> 00:03:38.930
if you do factorization
of this tensor,

00:03:38.931 --> 00:03:42.530
you can learn something
about what people are doing.

00:03:42.531 --> 00:03:45.350
This tensor is extremely sparse.

00:03:45.351 --> 00:03:50.500
It contains the whole tensor
is 15 quintillion zeros.

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:52.925
And that's enough that you need

00:03:52.926 --> 00:03:54.285
about 10 percent of

00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:56.951
all the disk space in
the world to store this tensor.

00:03:56.952 --> 00:04:05.920
But it only has 1.6 gigabytes
or about 1.7 billion zeros.

00:04:05.921 --> 00:04:08.860
So not so many zeros,
but for each non-zero,

00:04:08.861 --> 00:04:12.275
1.7 billion non-zeros.

00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:15.435
And for each non zero,
you have 10 billion zeros.

00:04:15.436 --> 00:04:18.245
There's no way you can store
this unless you compress it.

00:04:18.246 --> 00:04:22.370
So, you have to take
advantage of this sparsity.

00:04:22.371 --> 00:04:24.965
We have this kind of extreme
sparse that is quite common

00:04:24.966 --> 00:04:27.390
in data analytics and
science and engineering.

00:04:27.391 --> 00:04:28.760
It's all over the place.

00:04:28.761 --> 00:04:30.020
We're starting to see some break

00:04:30.021 --> 00:04:31.655
on sparse neural networks,

00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:32.710
but you have some
sparse in there.

00:04:32.711 --> 00:04:35.310
They haven't gotten to
this level of sparsity yet,

00:04:35.311 --> 00:04:36.610
but perhaps they should.

00:04:36.611 --> 00:04:39.420
Because the human brain has
about 100 billion neurons,

00:04:39.421 --> 00:04:40.790
and each neuron is
only connected to

00:04:40.791 --> 00:04:43.295
about 7,000 other neurons.

00:04:43.296 --> 00:04:45.285
That's some extreme
sparse system.

00:04:45.286 --> 00:04:47.030
So if you can model
something like that,

00:04:47.031 --> 00:04:49.950
we could have this kind
of sparsity there.

00:04:49.951 --> 00:04:53.420
So, one question is

00:04:53.421 --> 00:04:55.650
why we need a Tensor
Algebra Compiler.

00:04:55.651 --> 00:04:57.470
And I'm going to
argue that we do

00:04:57.471 --> 00:04:59.825
need a Tensor Algebra Compiler.

00:04:59.826 --> 00:05:04.610
So if you consider handwriting
this different expressions

00:05:04.611 --> 00:05:06.635
that you would need if you are

00:05:06.636 --> 00:05:09.785
working with linear algebra
or tensor algebra,

00:05:09.786 --> 00:05:12.010
the current approach with

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:14.015
traditional logs is
the we hand coded.

00:05:14.016 --> 00:05:15.581
So, this is a very
simple expression.

00:05:15.582 --> 00:05:17.085
It's matrix-vector
multiplication.

00:05:17.086 --> 00:05:19.189
It's sparse metrics-vector
multiplication.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.835
So, there is B, like
there, is sparse.

00:05:22.836 --> 00:05:25.075
The Eigen library implemented

00:05:25.076 --> 00:05:27.480
this operation,
they hand coded it.

00:05:27.481 --> 00:05:29.224
Then the CSparse library,

00:05:29.225 --> 00:05:31.185
implemented a different kernel,

00:05:31.186 --> 00:05:32.540
that in one kernel,

00:05:32.541 --> 00:05:35.945
it multiplies B by C
and then it adds it,

00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:37.725
it accumulates into the output.

00:05:37.726 --> 00:05:38.960
And they implemented this kernel

00:05:38.961 --> 00:05:40.790
because sometimes you
get better performance

00:05:40.791 --> 00:05:42.170
by doing the whole
operation at one go.

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:43.740
&gt;&gt; They didn't talk
about sparsity,

00:05:43.741 --> 00:05:45.720
just specifically
the matrices sparse

00:05:45.721 --> 00:05:46.993
and not the vectors.

00:05:46.994 --> 00:05:48.955
&gt;&gt; In this case.

00:05:48.956 --> 00:05:50.438
That will come, they
will be sparse too.

00:05:50.439 --> 00:05:51.750
But right now it's
only the matrices,

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.570
I suppose that's correct.

00:05:53.571 --> 00:05:56.750
So there's two different kernels
of people hand coded.

00:05:56.751 --> 00:05:58.230
Then you have PETSc,

00:05:58.231 --> 00:05:59.405
that figured out that you can

00:05:59.406 --> 00:06:01.490
add a different
vector to result.

00:06:01.491 --> 00:06:03.595
OSKI figured out that
sometimes you can get

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:07.495
better performance if you
also scale both sides.

00:06:07.496 --> 00:06:11.595
Then the PETSc learn that
you can also on the fly,

00:06:11.596 --> 00:06:13.305
transpose this matrix,

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:14.968
and then sometimes you want
to transpose the matrix,

00:06:14.969 --> 00:06:16.240
you want to add the vector.

00:06:16.241 --> 00:06:17.330
So someone wrote all these

00:06:17.331 --> 00:06:18.900
kernels because in
different context,

00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:21.975
these different kernels
give the best performance.

00:06:21.976 --> 00:06:25.490
So binary expression are
not enough because you loose

00:06:25.491 --> 00:06:29.955
performance due to
the temporal locality loss.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:31.700
But then you have
different sparse

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:33.405
formats for the matrix.

00:06:33.406 --> 00:06:35.480
These sparse formats
can be blocked.

00:06:35.481 --> 00:06:36.920
They can be a variable size

00:06:36.921 --> 00:06:37.760
blocked if you're dealing with

00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:40.185
something like
generalized coordinates.

00:06:40.186 --> 00:06:41.560
And because we
double compress it,

00:06:41.561 --> 00:06:44.815
so now you have 132 different
variants of this operation.

00:06:44.816 --> 00:06:46.498
Someone has to go and hand code.

00:06:46.499 --> 00:06:48.260
Then the vector can be dense or

00:06:48.261 --> 00:06:50.945
sparse which adds more variants,

00:06:50.946 --> 00:06:52.745
both output and input and

00:06:52.746 --> 00:06:54.778
this thing you're passing
through can be sparse.

00:06:54.779 --> 00:06:55.790
And then you have the rest of

00:06:55.791 --> 00:06:57.050
the tensor algebra expression.

00:06:57.051 --> 00:06:59.085
And there's an infinite
number of them.

00:06:59.086 --> 00:07:02.660
And even if you only consider
the binary operations,

00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:04.610
since the tensor
can be any order,

00:07:04.611 --> 00:07:06.960
you have an infinite number
of binary operations too.

00:07:06.961 --> 00:07:09.380
But I'll show this
very clearly later.

00:07:09.381 --> 00:07:11.030
That if you only consider
binary operations,

00:07:11.031 --> 00:07:12.875
you are losing a lot
of performance.

00:07:12.876 --> 00:07:17.300
You're loosing sometimes
asymptotically performance.

00:07:17.301 --> 00:07:22.300
So, we have a Tensor Algebra
Compiler called Taco.

00:07:22.301 --> 00:07:25.240
The alternative to
such a compiler is

00:07:25.241 --> 00:07:27.930
a traditional library where
you hand code these kernels.

00:07:27.931 --> 00:07:29.560
With that kind of
approach, you have to

00:07:29.561 --> 00:07:31.720
choose a few expressions
traditionally.

00:07:31.721 --> 00:07:33.510
So, you choose
the most important ones.

00:07:33.511 --> 00:07:36.490
With those by compiling,
you get an expression.

00:07:36.491 --> 00:07:38.260
You choose a few format with

00:07:38.261 --> 00:07:40.360
a traditional library that
you want to implement.

00:07:40.361 --> 00:07:42.415
Typically, CSR and CSC.

00:07:42.416 --> 00:07:43.510
Probably both.

00:07:43.511 --> 00:07:45.780
With the Tensor
Algebra Compiler,

00:07:45.781 --> 00:07:47.440
you get many formats.

00:07:47.441 --> 00:07:49.150
With traditional approach, you

00:07:49.151 --> 00:07:51.495
spend years hand
optimizing these kernels.

00:07:51.496 --> 00:07:53.005
And this guy has
spent many years.

00:07:53.006 --> 00:07:54.379
With the compiler, you can

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.465
relax because the compiler
will do it for you.

00:07:56.466 --> 00:07:59.480
This cat has a compiler.

00:07:59.481 --> 00:08:01.750
So, we're going to talk
the rest of this talk,

00:08:01.751 --> 00:08:02.230
I'm going to talk

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:02.866
about sparse code and
data to get a sense

00:08:02.867 --> 00:08:05.080
for sparsity and how

00:08:05.081 --> 00:08:07.380
the code looks like
when they're sparse.

00:08:07.381 --> 00:08:08.505
Then we're going to talk
about the intermediate

00:08:08.506 --> 00:08:10.880
representation and
code generation.

00:08:10.881 --> 00:08:12.520
What we do to automate

00:08:12.521 --> 00:08:15.539
generating these codes. Then
we're going to evaluate.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.830
But first, let's look at
some sparse code and data.

00:08:19.831 --> 00:08:24.290
So this is a fairly simple
tensor algebra expression.

00:08:24.291 --> 00:08:27.065
You have a tensor B,

00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:29.555
that you're multiplying
by a vector k,

00:08:29.556 --> 00:08:31.210
so you're summing
all this dimension.

00:08:31.211 --> 00:08:32.980
So, it's like a
matrix-vector multiplication

00:08:32.981 --> 00:08:34.360
with one more dimension.

00:08:34.361 --> 00:08:36.300
And the result is a matrix.

00:08:36.301 --> 00:08:38.515
So those variables
are called summation

00:08:38.516 --> 00:08:41.405
variables because you
sum over it. That's okay.

00:08:41.406 --> 00:08:42.495
I and j are called free

00:08:42.496 --> 00:08:45.580
variables because you
don't sum over them.

00:08:45.581 --> 00:08:47.110
So they are used on the left

00:08:47.111 --> 00:08:49.570
hand side of this expression.

00:08:49.571 --> 00:08:52.210
So if all of these
three tensors are dense,

00:08:52.211 --> 00:08:54.410
so you have a dense matrix,

00:08:54.411 --> 00:08:57.250
a dense three tensor,
and a dense vector.

00:08:57.251 --> 00:08:59.160
Then the code is
quite straightforward.

00:08:59.161 --> 00:09:00.700
We can write this code easily.

00:09:00.701 --> 00:09:02.200
You got three nested loops,

00:09:02.201 --> 00:09:03.550
and you do the accumulation and

00:09:03.551 --> 00:09:06.640
multiplication in the center
of these three loops.

00:09:06.641 --> 00:09:10.745
But if you want to start
making this [inaudible] sparse.

00:09:10.746 --> 00:09:12.370
So, in our system,

00:09:12.371 --> 00:09:13.720
you can choose
better attempts to

00:09:13.721 --> 00:09:15.355
the sparse per dimension.

00:09:15.356 --> 00:09:16.675
So for each dimension you say,

00:09:16.676 --> 00:09:17.815
this dimension is sparse,

00:09:17.816 --> 00:09:19.145
or this dimension is dense.

00:09:19.146 --> 00:09:21.725
That gives you many
combinations of formats.

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:23.860
Like there is some I think

00:09:23.861 --> 00:09:27.810
16 combinations for this
and 8 combinations for that.

00:09:27.811 --> 00:09:29.245
So if I make that dimension,

00:09:29.246 --> 00:09:31.240
I dimensions of these,

00:09:31.241 --> 00:09:32.650
the three tensors sparse,

00:09:32.651 --> 00:09:34.270
so I make it sparse.

00:09:34.271 --> 00:09:37.155
Then the code changes
a little bit there.

00:09:37.156 --> 00:09:40.265
So instead of it's rating
of the whole domain,

00:09:40.266 --> 00:09:42.715
it rates only all
the data structure,

00:09:42.716 --> 00:09:45.265
this sparse data structure
in that dimension of B.

00:09:45.266 --> 00:09:47.365
To make the next
dimension sparse,

00:09:47.366 --> 00:09:49.130
it changes the same way.

00:09:49.131 --> 00:09:50.510
To make this cell dimension

00:09:50.511 --> 00:09:51.925
sparse, it changes the same way.

00:09:51.926 --> 00:09:55.523
But if I want to make
this vector sparse,

00:09:55.524 --> 00:09:57.915
then I have to
insert quite a bit of

00:09:57.916 --> 00:10:00.865
code to notch
together this vector,

00:10:00.866 --> 00:10:01.940
this sparse vector with

00:10:01.941 --> 00:10:04.485
that sparse tensor,
in the k dimension.

00:10:04.486 --> 00:10:09.170
So that looks very similar to
nodes joining vector basis.

00:10:09.171 --> 00:10:11.840
If you're doing like inter-join.

00:10:11.841 --> 00:10:13.535
And if you have a summation,

00:10:13.536 --> 00:10:14.085
it'd look a lot

00:10:14.086 --> 00:10:14.840
like two-way notch
that Donald Knuth would

00:10:14.841 --> 00:10:19.175
describe in his book on
The Art of Computer Programming.

00:10:19.176 --> 00:10:20.830
That's using node sort
, I'm just using node

00:10:20.831 --> 00:10:22.740
showing some of the basis.

00:10:22.741 --> 00:10:24.200
We generate this kind of code,

00:10:24.201 --> 00:10:26.170
and we can generate
much more general code on this,

00:10:26.171 --> 00:10:28.565
and I'll show that.

00:10:28.566 --> 00:10:30.500
But suppose, what you

00:10:30.501 --> 00:10:31.850
want to do, you have
two sparse tensors,

00:10:31.851 --> 00:10:33.120
you want add them,

00:10:33.121 --> 00:10:36.665
you want them multiply by
vector in the same kernel.

00:10:36.666 --> 00:10:38.240
Then the code starts to blow up

00:10:38.241 --> 00:10:39.920
and you can't write
this code anymore,

00:10:39.921 --> 00:10:42.180
because there's all these cases
you have to consider.

00:10:42.181 --> 00:10:44.570
And I'll show quite a
straightforward theory

00:10:44.571 --> 00:10:47.006
for how to generate these cases.

00:10:47.007 --> 00:10:49.400
&gt;&gt; I have questions about

00:10:49.401 --> 00:10:52.305
when you say
the dimension is sparse.

00:10:52.306 --> 00:10:53.985
Does that order matter here?

00:10:53.986 --> 00:10:56.711
Like the order of dimensions
that you're talking about.

00:10:56.712 --> 00:10:56.936
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I left that out.

00:10:56.937 --> 00:10:59.113
You'll have to specify both,

00:10:59.114 --> 00:11:00.595
but each dimension is
dense or sparse and

00:11:00.596 --> 00:11:03.190
which order to store them.

00:11:03.191 --> 00:11:03.579
&gt;&gt; For example, for your tensor,

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:04.327
if the first
dimension is sparse,

00:11:04.328 --> 00:11:11.060
it means that
the entire matrix is gone.

00:11:11.061 --> 00:11:12.410
If you say like, something

00:11:12.411 --> 00:11:13.970
is missing from
the second dimension,

00:11:13.971 --> 00:11:15.260
it means that the row is gone.

00:11:15.261 --> 00:11:15.505
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:15.506 --> 00:11:17.065
&gt;&gt; And then if you were to cut.

00:11:17.066 --> 00:11:21.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly what you said.

00:11:21.531 --> 00:11:23.190
If this first
dimension is dense,

00:11:23.191 --> 00:11:25.585
like this whole slides
just disappears,

00:11:25.586 --> 00:11:27.350
and then that goes down.

00:11:27.351 --> 00:11:30.230
In our system, you
not only specify this,

00:11:30.231 --> 00:11:32.440
you also specify the order
you stored your dimensions in.

00:11:32.441 --> 00:11:34.130
So with some matrix,
you can store it

00:11:34.131 --> 00:11:36.305
in column order which is CAC,

00:11:36.306 --> 00:11:37.805
compressed box column, or

00:11:37.806 --> 00:11:40.035
ROAM order which is
compressed partial.

00:11:40.036 --> 00:11:42.045
You can specify both things.

00:11:42.046 --> 00:11:45.275
I just left it out
for simplicity.

00:11:45.276 --> 00:11:48.316
Okay. So let's look at this.

00:11:48.317 --> 00:11:52.582
So that's how Spark works. Sir.

00:11:52.583 --> 00:11:53.060
&gt;&gt; I'm curios.
Obviously, as a promise,

00:11:53.061 --> 00:11:55.290
it existed for
many years, right?

00:11:55.291 --> 00:11:56.750
And you know people
have worked on.

00:11:56.751 --> 00:11:58.490
So what is it that's
different now,

00:11:58.491 --> 00:12:02.390
that why people in the 90's
who were working on Fortran,

00:12:02.391 --> 00:12:05.631
and trying to do
the same kind of,

00:12:05.632 --> 00:12:06.855
I'm mean just curious,

00:12:06.856 --> 00:12:08.240
how has the problem

00:12:08.241 --> 00:12:10.790
changed over the course
of the last decade?

00:12:10.791 --> 00:12:12.210
&gt;&gt; I don't that
the problem has changed.

00:12:12.211 --> 00:12:15.845
I think its just we found
a new attack angle on it.

00:12:15.846 --> 00:12:18.660
A lot of people worked
on the dense problems,

00:12:18.661 --> 00:12:20.930
like the polyhedron model
and things like that.

00:12:20.931 --> 00:12:22.685
They've made a lot
of progress there.

00:12:22.686 --> 00:12:26.265
Some people like the Bernoulli
work from Keshav Pingali.

00:12:26.266 --> 00:12:28.765
And some work from people in

00:12:28.766 --> 00:12:30.350
Amsterdam tried to tackle

00:12:30.351 --> 00:12:33.375
this part problem,
but they got stuck.

00:12:33.376 --> 00:12:36.465
I don't know exactly why they
didn't solve the problem.

00:12:36.466 --> 00:12:38.295
I think they got stuck
in the complexity,

00:12:38.296 --> 00:12:41.565
they only did for
linear algebras.

00:12:41.566 --> 00:12:42.890
I think maybe that
was the problem.

00:12:42.891 --> 00:12:44.445
Because if you only do
it with linear algebra,

00:12:44.446 --> 00:12:45.780
you don't see the generality.

00:12:45.781 --> 00:12:47.180
You have to go to
higher dimensions

00:12:47.181 --> 00:12:48.525
to see the generality.

00:12:48.526 --> 00:12:49.760
Just like a human thing.

00:12:49.761 --> 00:12:51.268
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:12:51.269 --> 00:12:52.490
&gt;&gt; And I think they got stuck in

00:12:52.491 --> 00:12:54.500
the complexity of
the whole problem.

00:12:54.501 --> 00:12:56.120
But we figured out
how to do this,

00:12:56.121 --> 00:12:58.700
how to generate this kind
of code level by level.

00:12:58.701 --> 00:13:01.210
So we do it from
one dimension at a time.

00:13:01.211 --> 00:13:02.510
And then, you can
solve the problem

00:13:02.511 --> 00:13:04.195
locally in that dimension.

00:13:04.196 --> 00:13:06.020
And then, it's
a manageable problem.

00:13:06.021 --> 00:13:07.360
You still have to deal
with the matching,

00:13:07.361 --> 00:13:09.690
be able to solve that too.

00:13:09.691 --> 00:13:11.345
I think they tried to solve

00:13:11.346 --> 00:13:13.870
the generic code for
the whole thing at once.

00:13:13.871 --> 00:13:14.801
&gt;&gt; I see, and then
stranded in complexity.

00:13:14.802 --> 00:13:14.806
It is too complex.

00:13:14.807 --> 00:13:19.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I think maybe
what changed about

00:13:19.821 --> 00:13:22.220
the problem is that

00:13:22.221 --> 00:13:23.360
we thought about it as
tensors and then because

00:13:23.361 --> 00:13:27.990
its more generalized
you see that generality.

00:13:27.991 --> 00:13:31.100
&gt;&gt;Start with the more general,
started representation.

00:13:31.101 --> 00:13:33.425
&gt;&gt; We actually started first
just with linear algebra

00:13:33.426 --> 00:13:34.760
and the theory got

00:13:34.761 --> 00:13:36.650
simpler when we
generalize the tensors.

00:13:36.651 --> 00:13:38.860
Which is kind of cool.

00:13:38.861 --> 00:13:39.090
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:39.091 --> 00:13:43.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah. The most
related prior work

00:13:43.581 --> 00:13:46.797
is Keshav Pingali 's
Bernoulli work from the 90's.

00:13:46.798 --> 00:13:50.023
He was at Cornell at the time.

00:13:50.024 --> 00:13:53.390
So that was the
sparse and dense code.

00:13:53.391 --> 00:13:55.350
Now we're going to look at
the sparse and dense data.

00:13:55.351 --> 00:13:57.805
So this is the same pretensor
but I unrolled it

00:13:57.806 --> 00:14:01.080
from the three matrix slices
are shown here.

00:14:01.081 --> 00:14:02.625
So if this is a dense format,

00:14:02.626 --> 00:14:04.980
I lay it out in memory linearly.

00:14:04.981 --> 00:14:08.560
And notice that these
coordinates form a complete tree.

00:14:08.561 --> 00:14:10.350
And that is important because

00:14:10.351 --> 00:14:11.740
since they form a complete tree,

00:14:11.741 --> 00:14:15.240
I can locate them element.
I have random access.

00:14:15.241 --> 00:14:17.480
I can locate the element by
just computing this formula,

00:14:17.481 --> 00:14:19.155
which is just a
strident formula.

00:14:19.156 --> 00:14:22.005
And they get straight
down to the elements.

00:14:22.006 --> 00:14:24.390
So I have random access.
It's important property.

00:14:24.391 --> 00:14:27.680
But I waste a lot of data
because all these are empty.

00:14:27.681 --> 00:14:30.420
I don't want to store them. So I

00:14:30.421 --> 00:14:33.085
want a compressed storage
format. What do I do?

00:14:33.086 --> 00:14:35.180
I remove all the zeros.

00:14:35.181 --> 00:14:37.580
I shove all these non zeros
next to each other.

00:14:37.581 --> 00:14:39.480
Now I don't have a
complete three anymore.

00:14:39.481 --> 00:14:41.425
So these can't be kept implicit.

00:14:41.426 --> 00:14:43.205
We have to store
these in the cells.

00:14:43.206 --> 00:14:44.775
So I start by storing the first,

00:14:44.776 --> 00:14:45.898
and this is in I dimension.

00:14:45.899 --> 00:14:49.370
Then I store this
in j dimension.

00:14:49.371 --> 00:14:51.465
Then I store for each I,

00:14:51.466 --> 00:14:52.680
which Js corresponds to that I.

00:14:52.681 --> 00:14:55.633
Then I store the Ks,

00:14:55.634 --> 00:14:58.110
then for each J, IJ combination

00:14:58.111 --> 00:14:59.960
which k's correspond to that.

00:14:59.961 --> 00:15:02.715
And that gives me
quite a general format.

00:15:02.716 --> 00:15:04.860
So the compressed partial
format from matrices

00:15:04.861 --> 00:15:07.721
has one of these sets
of indices.

00:15:07.722 --> 00:15:11.550
If it was a doubly
compressed partial,

00:15:11.551 --> 00:15:12.795
you would have two sets.

00:15:12.796 --> 00:15:14.460
In tensors you
just keep going and

00:15:14.461 --> 00:15:16.855
you add one and one
and one on top of it.

00:15:16.856 --> 00:15:18.480
This is something
called the compressed

00:15:18.481 --> 00:15:20.280
sparse fiber format.

00:15:20.281 --> 00:15:21.504
We can generate code for this.

00:15:21.505 --> 00:15:22.740
And we can generate
code with some of

00:15:22.741 --> 00:15:24.745
the modals as well.
Standard combination.

00:15:24.746 --> 00:15:27.220
And then you have

00:15:27.221 --> 00:15:30.140
for each of these just which
value corresponds to it.

00:15:30.141 --> 00:15:32.055
That's just by position.

00:15:32.056 --> 00:15:34.534
You don't have random
access; you lost that.

00:15:34.535 --> 00:15:36.715
But typically when you compute,

00:15:36.716 --> 00:15:38.015
you can if you merge things

00:15:38.016 --> 00:15:39.740
together you don't
need random access.

00:15:39.741 --> 00:15:42.160
You just scan over
the whole format.

00:15:42.161 --> 00:15:44.760
So I'll show that. So
if you want to locate

00:15:44.761 --> 00:15:49.140
the value with the same value
as we located it earlier,

00:15:49.141 --> 00:15:51.780
then you have to
scan across that row.

00:15:51.781 --> 00:15:53.668
You have to consider
all the values in that row;

00:15:53.669 --> 00:15:57.050
you scan across the Js
until you find J zero.

00:15:57.051 --> 00:15:59.382
You consider all the Ks
for that J zero.

00:15:59.383 --> 00:16:01.770
Then you scan the K's
until you find it.

00:16:01.771 --> 00:16:03.645
You don't, so you lose sort of.

00:16:03.646 --> 00:16:05.290
You lose random access.

00:16:05.291 --> 00:16:07.590
You can do this
but it's expensive.

00:16:07.591 --> 00:16:09.240
And what this format
does is that it creates

00:16:09.241 --> 00:16:12.090
this dependency chain
through your index variables.

00:16:12.091 --> 00:16:13.530
And those index variables

00:16:13.531 --> 00:16:15.000
are going to correspond
to your loops.

00:16:15.001 --> 00:16:18.215
Now you have dependency
in your loops.

00:16:18.216 --> 00:16:20.316
This is a key observation,

00:16:20.317 --> 00:16:21.810
and we call this
an iteration graph.

00:16:21.811 --> 00:16:25.125
And I will show how
it works in general.

00:16:25.126 --> 00:16:27.315
So I'm now going to go into
intermediate representation

00:16:27.316 --> 00:16:29.235
and code generation,

00:16:29.236 --> 00:16:31.020
which is our compiler.

00:16:31.021 --> 00:16:34.045
This is our compiler,
so we call it TACO.

00:16:34.046 --> 00:16:38.460
That's an acronym for
tensor algebra compiler.

00:16:38.461 --> 00:16:40.123
The way it works
is that it takes in

00:16:40.124 --> 00:16:43.149
a tensor expression,
any tensor expression.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:44.815
It can be any number
of operands.

00:16:44.816 --> 00:16:46.440
And for each operand
you have to say

00:16:46.441 --> 00:16:49.460
the format that operand
That's all you say.

00:16:49.461 --> 00:16:51.190
Then it produces c code.

00:16:51.191 --> 00:16:52.470
It produces a kernel that

00:16:52.471 --> 00:16:55.155
computes that expression
in those formats.

00:16:55.156 --> 00:16:56.675
We also have a library
so we can load

00:16:56.676 --> 00:16:58.860
data on all of
those things as well.

00:16:58.861 --> 00:17:01.065
Internally it turns
your expression

00:17:01.066 --> 00:17:02.370
into an iteration graph.

00:17:02.371 --> 00:17:07.645
Then from the iteration graph,
it produces code.

00:17:07.646 --> 00:17:07.799
When it produces code,

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.324
it uses this concept
called a merge latice.

00:17:09.325 --> 00:17:10.803
That's a new we

00:17:10.804 --> 00:17:13.300
concept developed to deal
with these arbitrary nodules.

00:17:13.301 --> 00:17:14.775
Because you don't only

00:17:14.776 --> 00:17:16.340
want to deal with
the binary nodules.

00:17:16.341 --> 00:17:18.870
You want to deal with like
merging three operands,

00:17:18.871 --> 00:17:24.105
four operands,
any number of operands.

00:17:24.106 --> 00:17:34.170
So we are going to look at
how the iteration space look.

00:17:34.171 --> 00:17:36.110
And we're going to build
up to this iteration graph

00:17:36.111 --> 00:17:38.120
and see where the
iteration graph comes from.

00:17:38.121 --> 00:17:39.460
So it comes from these formats,

00:17:39.461 --> 00:17:42.170
but it also comes
from your expression.

00:17:42.171 --> 00:17:45.035
So if you want to
compute this expression,

00:17:45.036 --> 00:17:48.152
you have to iterate
all the combinations of I,

00:17:48.153 --> 00:17:49.850
J and K. So it's going
to be three loops,

00:17:49.851 --> 00:17:52.205
and you iterate
that whole expression.

00:17:52.206 --> 00:17:54.260
So you can think of that as

00:17:54.261 --> 00:17:56.075
three dimensions
in the polyhedron.

00:17:56.076 --> 00:17:58.475
So now were are moving
towards the polyhedron model.

00:17:58.476 --> 00:18:00.786
At each location
in this polyhedron,

00:18:00.787 --> 00:18:02.007
you are going to access B,

00:18:02.008 --> 00:18:04.560
so you are accessing B
through this polyhedron.

00:18:04.561 --> 00:18:08.230
So it is a three
dimensional rectangle here.

00:18:08.231 --> 00:18:10.037
But then you have
to access K as well.

00:18:10.038 --> 00:18:12.800
And since K is
only one dimensional,

00:18:12.801 --> 00:18:14.841
it's only one of these.

00:18:14.842 --> 00:18:17.868
But you're going to
access this for each I J.

00:18:17.869 --> 00:18:19.705
So you can think of
it as a replicating

00:18:19.706 --> 00:18:22.780
this C across
the whole iteration space.

00:18:22.781 --> 00:18:25.155
Then when you do the computation
you have to merge these

00:18:25.156 --> 00:18:28.510
together and compute
the multiplication.

00:18:28.511 --> 00:18:31.240
So this is the polyhedron model.

00:18:31.241 --> 00:18:33.075
At the heart of it you have

00:18:33.076 --> 00:18:35.995
these fine
polyhedrons like this,

00:18:35.996 --> 00:18:37.320
but there have no holes.

00:18:37.321 --> 00:18:39.954
Like they're
the dense polyhedrons

00:18:39.955 --> 00:18:43.130
and compiler community has
spent many years figuring out

00:18:43.131 --> 00:18:46.800
how to compile stuff like
this and we've made a lot of

00:18:46.801 --> 00:18:51.235
success with that in
the 80s and 90s and 2000s.

00:18:51.236 --> 00:18:53.280
But applications have
been moving towards

00:18:53.281 --> 00:18:55.635
sparse representations
and the compiler community

00:18:55.636 --> 00:18:57.017
has to catch up to that.

00:18:57.018 --> 00:18:59.085
We have to give
them some support.

00:18:59.086 --> 00:19:01.080
And we can think of this in

00:19:01.081 --> 00:19:04.355
a sparse way as
polyhedrons with hole.

00:19:04.356 --> 00:19:06.935
Now we have a sparse polyhedron.

00:19:06.936 --> 00:19:08.640
This creates some opportunities

00:19:08.641 --> 00:19:09.615
and some problems
that we have to solve.

00:19:09.616 --> 00:19:14.560
We don't know where these holes
are at compile time.

00:19:14.561 --> 00:19:16.815
But we cannot touch these holes

00:19:16.816 --> 00:19:18.630
because I mention in
[inaudible] tensor,

00:19:18.631 --> 00:19:19.710
if you touch these holes,

00:19:19.711 --> 00:19:22.275
you will never finish.

00:19:22.276 --> 00:19:23.700
So you have to
generate code that

00:19:23.701 --> 00:19:25.300
avoids touching the holes,

00:19:25.301 --> 00:19:26.935
but that can compute

00:19:26.936 --> 00:19:29.800
the values where
you have non zeroes.

00:19:29.801 --> 00:19:33.930
So I put back this
dependency chain through

00:19:33.931 --> 00:19:37.975
the iteration variables that
corresponds to this tensor.

00:19:37.976 --> 00:19:42.910
Going to have a similar
dependency chain for the C vector.

00:19:42.911 --> 00:19:44.280
So it's dependency chain here,

00:19:44.281 --> 00:19:46.580
and dependency chain
on the B vector.

00:19:46.581 --> 00:19:47.980
When I merge them together,

00:19:47.981 --> 00:19:50.120
I merge these
sparse polyhedrons.

00:19:50.121 --> 00:19:52.765
I also merge together
these two iteration graphs.

00:19:52.766 --> 00:19:54.660
So now we have this
dependency chain and you have

00:19:54.661 --> 00:19:57.685
a merge here. So
it's collision there.

00:19:57.686 --> 00:19:59.160
So if I want to do that

00:19:59.161 --> 00:20:00.750
multiplication I
have to intersect

00:20:00.751 --> 00:20:02.595
the two sparse polyhedrons

00:20:02.596 --> 00:20:05.495
AND then compute
the intersection,

00:20:05.496 --> 00:20:08.875
and that's this multiplication.

00:20:08.876 --> 00:20:12.120
Then finally I'm going to
add in A which is the result.

00:20:12.121 --> 00:20:15.045
There's a dependency
on the result too.

00:20:15.046 --> 00:20:18.810
&gt;&gt; So, what this
iteration graph shows is

00:20:18.811 --> 00:20:21.967
the dependencies imposed by
the sparse data structure,

00:20:21.968 --> 00:20:25.170
and we're going to use
this one for code generation.

00:20:25.171 --> 00:20:27.600
We can generate

00:20:27.601 --> 00:20:29.425
these iteration graphs
for any expression.

00:20:29.426 --> 00:20:31.410
It's a general
representation. And here are

00:20:31.411 --> 00:20:34.935
some examples from linear
algebra and tensor algebra.

00:20:34.936 --> 00:20:38.520
So this goes from the matrix
factor multiplication,

00:20:38.521 --> 00:20:40.425
all the way down.

00:20:40.426 --> 00:20:41.700
This is the entity character key

00:20:41.701 --> 00:20:42.390
that we use for
factorizing tensors.

00:20:42.391 --> 00:20:46.350
This is a blocked matrix
vector multiplication,

00:20:46.351 --> 00:20:48.495
which turns into
a four-tensor operation.

00:20:48.496 --> 00:20:50.025
In our representation, we just

00:20:50.026 --> 00:20:52.120
blocked matrices of four tensor.

00:20:52.121 --> 00:20:56.490
This more blocked is
a six tensor, so forth.

00:20:56.491 --> 00:20:58.493
So let's look at
this expression again,

00:20:58.494 --> 00:21:00.180
and now, we're going to
look at code generations.

00:21:00.181 --> 00:21:01.305
So we have the iteration graph.

00:21:01.306 --> 00:21:05.383
We generated that
from the formats,

00:21:05.384 --> 00:21:07.343
and the expression, and
we're going to generate code.

00:21:07.344 --> 00:21:09.430
So if the first dimension of B

00:21:09.431 --> 00:21:11.665
of stance, then
it's very simple.

00:21:11.666 --> 00:21:13.665
We can generate the dense loop.

00:21:13.666 --> 00:21:15.840
So this gets back to what I'm

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:17.760
talking about a little earlier,

00:21:17.761 --> 00:21:21.090
which is you can generate code
for each level separately.

00:21:21.091 --> 00:21:23.565
So this lab, I can ignore
everything down here.

00:21:23.566 --> 00:21:24.925
You have a recursive
code generator

00:21:24.926 --> 00:21:26.105
argued that's just
going to record

00:21:26.106 --> 00:21:30.015
some tests and generate
code for each level.

00:21:30.016 --> 00:21:33.475
The first level, this is dense.

00:21:33.476 --> 00:21:35.370
You can just connect this loop.

00:21:35.371 --> 00:21:36.975
Second level, if B will sparse,

00:21:36.976 --> 00:21:38.550
you're going to meet
the sparse loop.

00:21:38.551 --> 00:21:42.605
The complexity comes in
when you have a match.

00:21:42.606 --> 00:21:47.250
