00:45:19.766 --> 00:45:21.421
The next one will be tensor one.

00:45:21.422 --> 00:45:23.895
This is sampled dense-dense
matrix multiplication.

00:45:23.896 --> 00:45:25.600
It comes from data analytics.

00:45:25.601 --> 00:45:29.125
It's taken from
a PhD thesis at Berkeley.

00:45:29.126 --> 00:45:30.590
Your element y is multiply,

00:45:30.591 --> 00:45:32.430
this is you multiply
two matrices,

00:45:32.431 --> 00:45:34.245
and a then your element y
is multiply back,

00:45:34.246 --> 00:45:36.570
it's a sparse matrix.

00:45:36.571 --> 00:45:38.355
So, you have a dense matrix,

00:45:38.356 --> 00:45:40.395
you multiply it
by a dense matrix.

00:45:40.396 --> 00:45:43.195
That gives you 64
in the products.

00:45:43.196 --> 00:45:45.540
But your element y
was multiplied by

00:45:45.541 --> 00:45:48.390
a sparse matrix
which means that you

00:45:48.391 --> 00:45:51.120
only have to do

00:45:51.121 --> 00:45:53.160
this dot product wherever
you have a non-zero here.

00:45:53.161 --> 00:45:53.451
So, if I have a non-zero there,

00:45:53.452 --> 00:45:56.305
I have to do this dot product.

00:45:56.306 --> 00:45:57.995
But there's only
10 of these guys.

00:45:57.996 --> 00:46:00.535
I don't have to produce
this dot product.

00:46:00.536 --> 00:46:02.880
So that brings me now
to turn in the products.

00:46:02.881 --> 00:46:04.410
So, here you have to implement

00:46:04.411 --> 00:46:05.984
this whole thing in one kernel.

00:46:05.985 --> 00:46:07.140
If you implement this in two

00:46:07.141 --> 00:46:08.805
binary kernels and compose them,

00:46:08.806 --> 00:46:11.405
you'd lose a lot of performance.

00:46:11.406 --> 00:46:13.320
So, this shows that this is

00:46:13.321 --> 00:46:14.280
like a general thing that

00:46:14.281 --> 00:46:16.245
happens outside of
linear algebra too,

00:46:16.246 --> 00:46:20.250
where when you compose these
high performance libraries,

00:46:20.251 --> 00:46:21.720
the first library might do work,

00:46:21.721 --> 00:46:23.530
but the next library
throws away.

00:46:23.531 --> 00:46:25.430
That's one problem.
The next issue,

00:46:25.431 --> 00:46:27.885
if you can post
libraries without sort of

00:46:27.886 --> 00:46:31.780
doing this kind of approach
you lose temporal locality.

00:46:31.781 --> 00:46:33.660
And the third problem
is that you might end

00:46:33.661 --> 00:46:36.090
up emitting data
from one library.

00:46:36.091 --> 00:46:38.370
Then you have to reorganize
that data to go into

00:46:38.371 --> 00:46:41.080
the next library because it
expects a different layout.

00:46:41.081 --> 00:46:44.040
So, they don't
really compulsively.

00:46:44.041 --> 00:46:46.455
The problem here, I was talking
to [inaudible] and told them,

00:46:46.456 --> 00:46:47.525
so you heard about that earlier,

00:46:47.526 --> 00:46:51.780
is that we are sort

00:46:51.781 --> 00:46:56.625
of handwriting this specific
routines on workable layout,

00:46:56.626 --> 00:46:57.870
and then we're composing them

00:46:57.871 --> 00:46:59.740
with it by changing the data.

00:46:59.741 --> 00:47:02.430
But we believe that

00:47:02.431 --> 00:47:05.070
you need to shape the code
to adapt to the data.

00:47:05.071 --> 00:47:07.060
You don't adapt
the data to the code.

00:47:07.061 --> 00:47:09.420
You generate the code
for the data you have.

00:47:09.421 --> 00:47:10.800
And then when you compose
through libraries,

00:47:10.801 --> 00:47:12.540
you generate code
to use that format,

00:47:12.541 --> 00:47:15.640
next library generates code
to use the same format.

00:47:15.641 --> 00:47:17.720
So, you get friction free.

00:47:17.721 --> 00:47:20.760
This is a model example
of why you need that.

